#!/usr/bin/env -S ./compose.mk cmk compile
# cmk_pragma ::: { "kind": "plugin" } :::

'''
fmake: GNU Make with resident dispatch, built as a portable artifact.

A zygote parses a makefile once and forks a child per request arriving on a unix
socket, so a dispatched goal skips the parse.  The patch is embedded here and the
image builds on demand; nothing else in cmk depends on this module, and a consumer
acquires the binary by copying it out of the built image.

The image body is raw, so the pinned version and digest ride as docker build args
rather than make variables, and docker expands them.

The embedded patch is the same text as tests/experimental/resident/socket-mode.patch,
which the harness builds from; test_fmake_module_cmk.py pins them equal.
'''

from cmk import Dockerfile, dockerfs
import log

# Per-execution names a shared parse would freeze; the pid list takes the child's own.
rearm ?= _mk.run.id CMK_IO_STACK CMK_BRF_PREFIX CMK_SCRATCH FMAKE_CLIENT
rearm.pid ?= _cmk.pid

# A stale zygote answers a later run with the wrong program, so liveness is caller-facing status.
zygotes:
	'''Live zygotes, one per line, as pid and the program each is parked on.
	'''
	ps -axo pid=,args= \
	  | grep -E -- '--serve[[:space:]]' | grep -v grep \
	  | sed -e 's/ *\([0-9]*\) .*-f *\([^ ]*\).*/\1 \2/' || true

reap:
	'''Stop every zygote and drop the sockets they left, including stale ones.
	'''
	log(${@} ${sep} reaping zygotes)
	pkill -f -- '--serve /tmp/cmk-zygote' 2>/dev/null || true
	rm -f /tmp/cmk-zygote.*.sock 2>/dev/null || true
	rmdir /tmp/cmk-zygote.*.sock.lock 2>/dev/null || true

# Elixir's own base, so the binary is glibc-compatible with any consumer built on it.
Dockerfile fmake(|
  FROM debian:trixie AS build
  ARG MAKE_VERSION=4.4.1
  ARG MAKE_SHA256=dd16fb1d67bfab79a72f5e8390735c49e3e8e70b4945a15ab1f81ddb78658fb3
  ARG MAKE_URL=https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/make
  RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends build-essential curl ca-certificates
  RUN curl -fsSL ${MAKE_URL}/make-${MAKE_VERSION}.tar.gz -o /tmp/make.tar.gz \
   && echo "${MAKE_SHA256}  /tmp/make.tar.gz" | sha256sum -c - \
   && tar xzf /tmp/make.tar.gz -C /tmp \
   && cd /tmp/make-${MAKE_VERSION} \
   && patch -p1 -l < /tmp/socket-mode.patch \
   && ./configure --disable-dependency-tracking CFLAGS="-O2" \
   && make -j`nproc` \
   && install -m 0755 make /usr/local/bin/fmake
  FROM debian:trixie-slim
  COPY --from=build /usr/local/bin/fmake /usr/local/bin/fmake
  ENTRYPOINT ["fmake", "--resident"]
|)

# The patch rides into the build context, so the image body stays free of copy lines.
dockerfs fmake.diff(bind=fmake, path=/tmp/socket-mode.patch, mode=0644)(|
Resident dispatch (socket mode) for GNU Make 4.4.1.

Parse once in a zygote, then fork a child per request arriving on a unix
socket. The parent never executes a goal, so it stays pristine and needs no
state reset; each child is an ordinary make from the hook point onward.

Two roles, both argv flags, so nothing in the environment triggers either:

  fmake --serve  /tmp/prog.sock -f twin.mk    parse once, listen, fork per request
  fmake --client /tmp/prog.sock -- make -f twin.mk V=1 target

Everything after -- is the caller's own cold command. The client sends it
without the program name, since the zygote already is that program, and keeps
the whole form to exec if no zygote can serve the request. Goals may also be
given bare, with FMAKE_COLD naming the make to prepend on fallback.

The child decides what it can serve, so all routing policy lives here rather
than at the call site. A zygote's options are its argv plus MAKEFLAGS as it
stands after the parse, which is what a recursive call site expands to, so a
zygote started with no flags at all still matches its own call sites. Short
flags compare letter by letter, since MAKEFLAGS spells a cluster as one word
and a request may ask for less than the zygote carries. Any option the zygote
lacks, a different makefile included, is answered with status 75 and the client
execs the cold command it is already holding. A missing socket and a refused
connection answer the same way.

A request carries the client's working directory, its argv, its environment,
and its three standard descriptors, passed as ancillary data. The child adopts
all of them, so pipelines, command substitution and concurrent requests work
with no routing convention. A reply is two lines on the same connection: the
child's pid, then its exit status. Pid zero means the parent answered without
forking.

The parent forks before it reads, so a client that stalls blocks only its own
child. It never blocks on a child either, so a recipe may dispatch back into
the zygote. The child reports its own status from inside die(), the only point
that catches both a normal return and a failing goal.

A zygote does not watch its inputs by default, because cmk names its artifacts
by content: a changed program is a different path, so the parse a zygote holds
cannot go stale under it, and watching there misfires when a warm cache touches
a file it read. FMAKE_WATCH arms the check for a makefile nothing else
version-stamps.

Applies to make-4.4.1.tar.gz, sha256
dd16fb1d67bfab79a72f5e8390735c49e3e8e70b4945a15ab1f81ddb78658fb3.
Apply with -l, since blank context lines carry no trailing space.

Four behaviours worth knowing. A child must not run remove_intermediates, since
it shares intermediate files with its parent, and a failing goal reaches die()
rather than returning, so that guard is required rather than defensive. Client
mode is settled at the top of main, before output is initialized, so its
failures report through a plain write to stderr instead of make's own path. The
client ignores a broken pipe for its own lifetime, because make installs a fatal
handler for it and a zygote may answer and close while the request is still in
flight; the default disposition is restored before the cold exec. And a signal
the client inherited as ignored stays ignored, as it does in make, so a zygote
started as a background job passes an ignored interrupt to its children.

Design notes and hazards are in scratch/SPIKE-resident-cmk.md.

--- a/src/file.c
+++ b/src/file.c
@@ -364,8 +364,10 @@
   struct file **file_end;
   int doneany = 0;
 
-  /* If there's no way we will ever remove anything anyway, punt early.  */
-  if (question_flag || touch_flag || all_secondary || no_intermediates)
+  /* If there's no way we will ever remove anything anyway, punt early.
+     A resident dispatch child shares these files with its parent.  */
+  if (question_flag || touch_flag || all_secondary || no_intermediates
+      || fmake_child)
     return;
 
   if (sig && just_print_flag)
